WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:06.885
This is the rapid-fire track: every answer in this episode, back to back, one breath each.

2
00:00:06.885 --> 00:00:10.717
No teaching, just the points in the order you would write them.

3
00:00:10.717 --> 00:00:14.549
It is made for the night before, and for the bus on the way in.

4
00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:18.199
If one of these sounds unfamiliar, go back to its own track.

5
00:00:19.649 --> 00:00:20.865
<v Examiner>Social determinants.

6
00:00:21.315 --> 00:00:27.386
Social determinants: the conditions in which people are born, grow, work, live and age.

7
00:00:27.386 --> 00:00:29.340
Name one, then give its why.

8
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.434
Income and the social gradient: lower status, worse health, shorter life.

9
00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:39.738
Stress: chronic fight or flight harms the heart, immunity and mental health.

10
00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:42.599
Education: jobs, income, health literacy.

11
00:00:42.599 --> 00:00:46.925
Early life: childhood trauma leads to mental illness, obesity,

12
00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:49.228
later heart disease and diabetes.

13
00:00:50.478 --> 00:00:51.737
<v Examiner>Equity and inequity.

14
00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:54.291
Equality: everyone gets the same.

15
00:00:54.291 --> 00:00:57.733
Equity: fairness, resources by need, to close the gap.

16
00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:01.048
Inequality: any difference in health between groups.

17
00:01:01.048 --> 00:01:05.894
Inequity: a difference that is unfair and avoidable, from social conditions,

18
00:01:05.894 --> 00:01:08.189
and fixable with the right policies.

19
00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:13.735
Example: Māori and Pacific adults twice as likely not to collect a prescription because

20
00:01:13.735 --> 00:01:14.245
of cost.

21
00:01:15.495 --> 00:01:16.797
<v Examiner>Levels of prevention.

22
00:01:17.247 --> 00:01:22.268
Primordial: stop risk factors developing, the social determinants, upstream,

23
00:01:22.268 --> 00:01:23.524
like Healthy Homes.

24
00:01:23.524 --> 00:01:27.951
Primary, prevent: vaccination and education, upstream to midstream.

25
00:01:27.951 --> 00:01:32.114
Secondary, screen: early detection, midstream, like mammograms.

26
00:01:32.114 --> 00:01:37.135
Tertiary, treat: manage existing disease, downstream, like diabetes support.

27
00:01:37.135 --> 00:01:38.985
Work at every level at once.

28
00:01:40.235 --> 00:01:41.473
<v Examiner>Population health.

29
00:01:41.923 --> 00:01:46.484
Population health: outcomes across a group, actions for the group.

30
00:01:46.484 --> 00:01:49.594
Personal health: one person, one-to-one care.

31
00:01:49.594 --> 00:01:55.123
Demography: size and structure of a population, fertility, mortality, migration.

32
00:01:55.123 --> 00:02:00.998
Epidemiology: patterns, causes and control of disease, observational or experimental.

33
00:02:00.998 --> 00:02:05.283
Endemic is the expected level; epidemic is more than expected.

34
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:07.578
<v Examiner>Health screening.

35
00:02:08.028 --> 00:02:13.326
Screening finds risk or disease before symptoms, to reduce illness and death.

36
00:02:13.326 --> 00:02:18.969
Organised, by the National Screening Unit: antenatal, newborn heel prick, hearing,

37
00:02:18.969 --> 00:02:23.786
cervical, breast from forty-five to sixty-nine every two years, bowel.

38
00:02:23.786 --> 00:02:27.020
Opportunistic: S T I, prostate, blood pressure.

39
00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.906
Barriers: cost, transport, fear of the unknown, English-only resources.

40
00:02:31.906 --> 00:02:34.383
Fixes: mobile clinics, self-testing.

41
00:02:34.383 --> 00:02:37.135
False negative: has it, told they don't.

42
00:02:38.385 --> 00:02:39.623
<v Examiner>Primary health care.

43
00:02:40.073 --> 00:02:45.408
Five principles of primary health care: accessibility, community participation,

44
00:02:45.408 --> 00:02:50.136
health promotion, appropriate technology, intersectoral collaboration.

45
00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:55.877
Health promotion continuum: education, screening, community action, social marketing,

46
00:02:55.877 --> 00:02:57.161
influencing policy.

47
00:02:58.411 --> 00:02:59.627
<v Examiner>The Health Strategy.

48
00:03:00.077 --> 00:03:05.018
Health Strategy 2023, under the Pae Ora, Healthy Futures, Act 2022.

49
00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:06.199
Vision: pae ora.

50
00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:11.804
Two goals: health equity, especially for Māori, Pacific and disabled people;

51
00:03:11.804 --> 00:03:13.869
and better outcomes for all.

52
00:03:13.869 --> 00:03:20.213
Six priorities: voice at the heart; flexible, appropriate care; valuing our workforce;

53
00:03:20.213 --> 00:03:24.269
a learning culture; a resilient and sustainable system;

54
00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:27.072
partnerships for health and wellbeing.

55
00:03:28.322 --> 00:03:29.261
<v Examiner>Te Tiriti.

56
00:03:29.711 --> 00:03:35.019
Te Tiriti articles: one, kāwanatanga, governance; two, tino rangatiratanga,

57
00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:38.345
autonomy and taonga; three, ōritetanga, equity.

58
00:03:38.345 --> 00:03:44.077
Five principles: tino rangatiratanga, mana taurite, whakamarumarutia, kōwhiringa,

59
00:03:44.077 --> 00:03:44.856
pātuitanga.

60
00:03:44.856 --> 00:03:46.696
The three Ps are outdated.

61
00:03:46.696 --> 00:03:50.447
Cultural safety: the patient decides if care is safe.

62
00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:52.934
<v Examiner>Competence and scope.

63
00:03:53.384 --> 00:03:58.138
Six pou: Māori health; cultural safety; whanaungatanga and communication;

64
00:03:58.138 --> 00:04:02.306
pūkengatanga and evidence; manaakitanga and people-centred care;

65
00:04:02.306 --> 00:04:04.260
rangatiratanga and leadership.

66
00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.795
Student scope: under an R N's supervision, only what you've been taught and assessed.

67
00:04:09.795 --> 00:04:11.488
Decline a telephone order.

68
00:04:11.488 --> 00:04:13.181
Decline giving meds alone.

69
00:04:14.431 --> 00:04:15.946
<v Examiner>Therapeutic use of self.

70
00:04:16.396 --> 00:04:18.661
Emotional intelligence: recognise,

71
00:04:18.661 --> 00:04:23.125
understand and manage your own emotions while responding to others.

72
00:04:23.125 --> 00:04:27.655
Self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy, social skills.

73
00:04:27.655 --> 00:04:33.051
Impact: empathy, trust, understanding needs, patient-centred care, less conflict,

74
00:04:33.051 --> 00:04:34.117
better outcomes.

75
00:04:34.117 --> 00:04:39.713
Therapeutic use of self rests on competent communication and emotional intelligence.

76
00:04:40.963 --> 00:04:42.243
<v Examiner>Family-centred care.

77
00:04:42.693 --> 00:04:48.377
Family: whoever the person says is family, with or without biological or legal ties.

78
00:04:48.377 --> 00:04:53.114
Three principles of partnership: relationship, genuine and respectful;

79
00:04:53.114 --> 00:04:59.001
participation and involvement, respecting autonomy; tailoring care to needs, strengths,

80
00:04:59.001 --> 00:05:00.286
limits and context.

81
00:05:00.286 --> 00:05:02.384
Core: empowerment and advocacy.

82
00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:04.658
<v Examiner>Pain assessment.

83
00:05:05.108 --> 00:05:07.595
Pain is what the person says it is.

84
00:05:07.595 --> 00:05:11.858
Acute: rapid onset, under six months, resolves with healing.

85
00:05:11.858 --> 00:05:16.051
Chronic: over six months, can bring depression and anxiety.

86
00:05:16.051 --> 00:05:22.446
Assess with OLD CARTS: onset, location, duration, characteristics, aggravating, relieving,

87
00:05:22.446 --> 00:05:23.867
treatment, severity.

88
00:05:23.867 --> 00:05:26.851
Zero-to-ten scale at rest and on movement;

89
00:05:26.851 --> 00:05:30.546
Wong-Baker faces for children or a language barrier.

90
00:05:31.796 --> 00:05:33.033
<v Examiner>Discharge planning.

91
00:05:33.483 --> 00:05:37.167
Discharge planning starts at admission: interdisciplinary,

92
00:05:37.167 --> 00:05:42.249
for continuity and a smooth transition; it cuts readmissions and length of stay.

93
00:05:42.249 --> 00:05:43.392
Nurse coordinates.

94
00:05:43.392 --> 00:05:45.552
Assess, plan, implement, evaluate.

95
00:05:45.552 --> 00:05:50.634
Include: how they get home, medicines, diet, wounds, pain, equipment, follow-up,

96
00:05:50.634 --> 00:05:54.000
lifting and driving, warning signs, health promotion.

97
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.033
Check they understand and agree.

98
00:05:57.283 --> 00:05:58.541
<v Examiner>The nursing process.

99
00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:05.201
Nursing process, A D P I E: assessment, with objective signs and subjective symptoms;

100
00:06:05.201 --> 00:06:11.703
diagnosis, N A N D A, prioritised; planning, SMART goals; implementation, evidence-based,

101
00:06:11.703 --> 00:06:17.328
safe, in scope; evaluation, met, partly met or not met, timed, dated, signed.

102
00:06:17.328 --> 00:06:19.958
Medical diagnosis names the disease;

103
00:06:19.958 --> 00:06:24.195
nursing diagnosis names the response the nurse can manage.

104
00:06:25.445 --> 00:06:26.511
<v Examiner>Falls and wounds.

105
00:06:26.961 --> 00:06:32.089
Falls, Lisa's marking: one mark identify five, one describe, two explain.

106
00:06:32.089 --> 00:06:37.286
Assess with Morse; clear the environment; call bell in reach and teach it;

107
00:06:37.286 --> 00:06:41.501
mobility aids with physio; help to mobilise, non-slip socks.

108
00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:46.698
They reduce injury, keep independence, prevent fractures and longer stays.

109
00:06:46.698 --> 00:06:49.157
Morse over forty-five is high risk.

110
00:06:49.157 --> 00:06:53.722
Wounds: TIME, tissue, inflammation or infection, moisture, edges.

111
00:06:54.972 --> 00:06:56.145
<v Examiner>Informed consent.

112
00:06:56.595 --> 00:07:01.442
Informed consent: benefits, risks and alternatives, so the patient can decide;

113
00:07:01.442 --> 00:07:03.617
every procedure, written or verbal.

114
00:07:03.617 --> 00:07:08.339
Right seven of the Code; Bill of Rights section eleven, the right to refuse.

115
00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:10.824
Sixteen and over consent for themselves.

116
00:07:10.824 --> 00:07:15.546
Nurse is the advocate: professional interpreter, whānau, call the team back,

117
00:07:15.546 --> 00:07:16.789
check understanding.

118
00:07:16.789 --> 00:07:19.337
Withdrawn mid-procedure: stop and notify.

119
00:07:20.587 --> 00:07:21.611
<v Examiner>Health literacy.

120
00:07:22.061 --> 00:07:27.519
Health literacy: the ability to find, understand and use health information and services;

121
00:07:27.519 --> 00:07:29.542
not about intelligence or income.

122
00:07:29.542 --> 00:07:32.670
Low literacy means less screening, more admissions.

123
00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.798
Teach-back is the gold standard: Ask, Build, Check,

124
00:07:35.798 --> 00:07:38.189
"tell and show me what you understand".

125
00:07:38.189 --> 00:07:43.095
Interpreter, plain language, three to five key points, demonstrate, be positive.

126
00:07:44.345 --> 00:07:45.391
<v Examiner>Vital signs.

127
00:07:45.841 --> 00:07:49.633
Primary survey: airway; breathing, twelve to twenty,

128
00:07:49.633 --> 00:07:53.426
saturations ninety-five to one hundred; circulation,

129
00:07:53.426 --> 00:07:59.552
B P one-ten to one-forty over sixty to ninety, pulse sixty to a hundred; disability,

130
00:07:59.552 --> 00:08:02.616
AVPU and G C S three to fifteen; exposure,

131
00:08:02.616 --> 00:08:06.189
thirty-six point five to thirty-seven point five.

132
00:08:06.189 --> 00:08:08.596
Glucose four to seven point five.

133
00:08:08.596 --> 00:08:13.775
Injections: I M ninety degrees, subcut forty-five, intradermal fifteen.

134
00:08:15.025 --> 00:08:16.646
<v Examiner>Documentation and ISBAR.

135
00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:23.169
Documentation is legal, the H P C A Act, the Code and the Privacy Act; professional,

136
00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:27.001
the Code of Conduct and pou three; and communication.

137
00:08:27.001 --> 00:08:29.170
Not documented means not done.

138
00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:32.929
Blue or black ink, no gaps, one line through errors.

139
00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:38.351
ISBAR: identify, situation, background, assessment, recommendation, review.

140
00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:44.497
SOAPIER: subjective, objective, assessment, plan, intervention, evaluation, revision.

141
00:08:45.747 --> 00:08:46.984
<v Examiner>Infection control.

142
00:08:47.434 --> 00:08:52.856
Chain of infection: agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission,

143
00:08:52.856 --> 00:08:55.314
portal of entry, susceptible host.

144
00:08:55.314 --> 00:09:01.531
Standard precautions for everyone: hand hygiene first, P P E, cough etiquette, sharps,

145
00:09:01.531 --> 00:09:03.338
waste, aseptic technique.

146
00:09:03.338 --> 00:09:06.591
Contact: gown and gloves, M R S A and gastro.

147
00:09:06.591 --> 00:09:08.398
Droplet: mask, influenza.

148
00:09:08.398 --> 00:09:14.109
Airborne: N ninety-five and a negative-pressure room, measles, chickenpox, T B.

149
00:09:15.359 --> 00:09:16.853
<v Examiner>Evidence-based practice.

150
00:09:17.303 --> 00:09:22.415
Evidence-based practice: best research, clinical expertise and patient values,

151
00:09:22.415 --> 00:09:23.726
to improve outcomes.

152
00:09:23.726 --> 00:09:26.675
Required by pou four and the Code of Conduct.

153
00:09:26.675 --> 00:09:31.787
Evidence from weakest to strongest: expert opinion, qualitative, quantitative,

154
00:09:31.787 --> 00:09:37.227
non-randomised trials, randomised controlled trials, systematic reviews at the top.

155
00:09:37.227 --> 00:09:39.980
First step: formulate a clinical question.

156
00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:42.062
<v Examiner>Self-care.

157
00:09:42.512 --> 00:09:47.967
Burnout: from long-term workplace stress; exhaustion, cynicism, reduced efficacy;

158
00:09:47.967 --> 00:09:50.526
compassion survives; builds over time.

159
00:09:50.526 --> 00:09:55.576
Compassion fatigue: from absorbing patients' suffering as secondary trauma;

160
00:09:55.576 --> 00:09:58.135
loss of empathy; can follow one event.

161
00:09:58.135 --> 00:10:02.580
Self-care is deliberate, enjoyable, not selfish, needs boundaries;

162
00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.869
it builds resilience against both.

163
00:10:06.119 --> 00:10:07.399
<v Examiner>Long-term conditions.

164
00:10:07.849 --> 00:10:13.367
Long-term conditions: ongoing or recurring, often several at once, multimorbidity.

165
00:10:13.367 --> 00:10:18.077
Self-management: the person manages it themselves, every day, ongoing.

166
00:10:18.077 --> 00:10:22.047
Nurse's role: information for informed decisions and goals,

167
00:10:22.047 --> 00:10:27.699
treat them as experts in their own lives, groups, cultural fit, follow-up, referral.

168
00:10:27.699 --> 00:10:29.247
The word is empowering.

169
00:10:30.497 --> 00:10:32.417
<v Examiner>Diabetes and rheumatic fever.

170
00:10:32.867 --> 00:10:38.808
Type 1 diabetes: autoimmune, children, no insulin; polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia,

171
00:10:38.808 --> 00:10:39.657
weight loss.

172
00:10:39.657 --> 00:10:45.457
Type 2: insulin resistance, lifestyle and genes, plus infections and slow healing.

173
00:10:45.457 --> 00:10:48.993
Glucose four to seven point five; hypo under four.

174
00:10:48.993 --> 00:10:55.076
Rheumatic fever: autoimmune, after untreated strep throat; Māori and Pacific children,

175
00:10:55.076 --> 00:11:00.310
crowded housing; penicillin every twenty-eight days; can damage the heart.

176
00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:02.584
<v Examiner>Clinical facts.

177
00:11:03.034 --> 00:11:08.820
Clinical keys: thirty-two breaths is tachypnoea; forty-eight beats is bradycardia;

178
00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:12.418
under thirty-five is hypothermia; fever is pyrexia.

179
00:11:12.418 --> 00:11:14.182
P R N pain: assess first.

180
00:11:14.182 --> 00:11:17.781
Donning: hand hygiene, gown, mask, goggles, gloves.

181
00:11:17.781 --> 00:11:21.379
Black pen; an error gets one line, initials, error.

182
00:11:21.379 --> 00:11:23.990
Frequent hypos means revise the plan.

183
00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:25.754
Gillick competence: true.

184
00:11:27.004 --> 00:11:29.031
<v Examiner>Population and professional facts.

185
00:11:29.481 --> 00:11:34.728
Population and professional keys: whooping cough is pertussis; incidence rate;

186
00:11:34.728 --> 00:11:39.437
equity is tailoring to need; Code of Conduct principle one is dignity,

187
00:11:39.437 --> 00:11:44.213
principle eight is public trust; family disagrees, the nurse advocates;

188
00:11:44.213 --> 00:11:49.998
three conditions at once is multimorbidity; a relative's report is a secondary source;

189
00:11:49.998 --> 00:11:54.236
heart sounds are objective; hand hygiene is the most effective.

190
00:11:55.486 --> 00:11:56.624
That's every answer.

191
00:11:56.624 --> 00:11:57.534
Go and get them.
